---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const markowitzCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'corr', label: 'correlation between assets', min: -0.5, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.3 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'single-asset volatility (both)', expr: '20' },
    { label: '50/50 portfolio volatility', expr: 'sqrt((20^2 + 20^2 + 2*corr*20*20))/2' },
    { label: 'risk reduction achieved', expr: '100 - (sqrt((20^2 + 20^2 + 2*corr*20*20))/2)/20*100', unit: '%' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/advanced/portfolio-theory-markowitz">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Markowitz's 1952 insight won the Nobel and rebuilt finance: <strong>portfolio risk depends not
    just on each asset's risk but on how they move together</strong>. Combining imperfectly
    correlated assets produces portfolios better than any ingredient — and plotting all possible
    combinations reveals the <strong>efficient frontier</strong>: the best return available at
    each level of risk.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the correlation dial — watch the free lunch shrink as assets move together" widget="sliderLab" config={markowitzCfg} />

  <p>
    With correlation 0.3, a 50/50 mix carries ~11.7% volatility instead of 20% — a 41% risk
    reduction from diversifying alone. Perfect correlation (1.0) gives zero benefit. That dial is
    the entire argument for international diversification, bond mixing, and every "uncorrelated
    asset" pitch you will ever hear.
  </p>

  <h2>From theory to practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The efficient frontier:</strong> for every risk level, one portfolio maximises expected return. Rational investors pick a point on it — their choice of point is their risk appetite, made precise.</li>
    <li><strong>The Two-Fund separation:</strong> every efficient portfolio mixes just two things — the risk-free asset and one optimal risky portfolio. Simplicity hidden inside the mathematics.</li>
    <li><strong>The estimation problem:</strong> the model needs expected returns (unobservable) and correlations (unstable) — "error maximisation" (Michaud): the optimizer overweights whatever was measured wrongly. Robust estimation and simple allocations are the workarounds.</li>
    <li><strong>Its Nobel legacy:</strong> CAPM (Sharpe) and factor models grew directly out of Markowitz's framework — the entire asset-pricing field is this lesson's children.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Markowitz, Journal of Finance (1952, Nobel 1990); Elton & Gruber, Modern Portfolio Theory; Michaud, Efficient Asset Management.</p>
</TopicPage>
